MGNT110 — Introduction to Leadership (Lecture 4)

What is Leadership?

A relationship, a process of influence — not just a position
Manager = authority; Leader = influence

History of Leadership

Classical — leaders chosen by birthright or divine right
Renaissance (Machiavelli) — leadership as power and control
Modern — rise of corporations, still shaped by "Great Man" thinking (e.g. corporate dynasties like Samsung)
Postmodern — leadership is socially constructed, contextual, and culturally influenced

Shift: From "born leaders" → "context matters" | From top-down hero → shared & relational

Leadership Theories
Trait Theory

Leaders are born with traits like confidence, intelligence, charisma
Limitation: ignores context; having traits doesn't guarantee becoming a leader

Behavioural Theory

Leaders defined by what they do, not who they are
Two types: task-oriented vs people-oriented
Key idea: leadership can be learned


Relational & Contemporary Leadership
Leadership as Relationship

Co-produced by leaders and followers through trust, communication, and emotional intelligence

Distributed Leadership

Responsibility spread across the team, not monopolised by one person
Influence flows in multiple directions — not a straight line

Leader–Member Exchange (LMX)

Based on the quality of the relationship between leader and each follower
Risk: favouritism, resentment, and lower morale in the "out-group"

Transformational vs Transactional
TransactionalTransformationalRewards/punishmentsInspires & motivatesMonitors tasksBuilds meaningEnsures deadlinesFocuses on people
Passive-Avoidant Leadership

Passive (reactive): only acts when problems become serious
Avoidant (laissez-faire): completely avoids decision-making and involvement


Modern Perspectives
Leadership is Contextual

No single best style — depends on people, situation, environment, team, and culture

Emotional Intelligence (EI)

Ability to understand emotions, manage relationships, and communicate effectively
High EI → better trust-building, conflict resolution, team motivation

Leader as Coach

Modern leaders guide, develop skills, support growth, and help people perform better — not just give instructions

Positive Leadership

Builds confidence (self-efficacy), motivation, and psychological capital
Leads to better stress handling, resilience, and performance


Key Takeaways

Leadership = influence + relationships
It is contextual — no "one best style"
Modern leadership is shared, dynamic, and relational